# The control-socket directory is created TWICE, and only the first creation
# gets its label from file_contexts.
#
# init.mt6755.rc's `on post-fs-data` mkdirs /data/vendor/wifi/sock and
# restorecons it, so at boot it is wpa_data_file. But wpa_supplicant owns that
# directory for the lifetime of the interface: ctrl_iface_unix.c rmdir()s it in
# wpa_supplicant_ctrl_iface_deinit() and mkdir()s it again on the next
# ctrl_iface_init(). A directory created at runtime takes its parent's type,
# not file_contexts, so after the first Wi-Fi off/on cycle the directory and
# the wlan0 socket inside it were both vendor_data_file again. Measured on the
# live image: nine distinct denials, 36 records, all of them after 23:21 while
# /data/vendor/wifi/wpa from 14:19 still carried the right label.
#
# A named type_transition is how AOSP solves the identical problem one
# directory over -- system/sepolicy/vendor/hal_wifi_supplicant_default.te:8
# does `type_transition hal_wifi_supplicant_default wifi_data_file:dir
# wpa_socket "sockets"` for the legacy /data/misc path. This is the same rule
# for this port's path.
#
# What is deliberately NOT granted: vendor_data_file sock_file create, or any
# file access on vendor_data_file. With the transition in place the socket is
# created inside a wpa_data_file directory and inherits that type, so AOSP's
# own wpa_data_file:sock_file create_file_perms covers it. Granting the generic
# type would let wpa_supplicant put a socket anywhere under /data/vendor.
# Scoped to /data/vendor/wifi through its own type rather than to the generic
# vendor_data_file -- see connectivity/file.te. `search` is restated because
# public/domain.te's blanket vendor_data_file:dir { getattr search } does not
# reach the new type.
allow hal_wifi_supplicant_default mtk_wifi_vendor_data_file:dir
    { getattr search write add_name remove_name };
type_transition hal_wifi_supplicant_default mtk_wifi_vendor_data_file:dir wpa_data_file "sock";
